# Spec-Driven Development (SDD)

> Unlike `architecture.md`/`conventions.md`/`verification.md`, this file has no TODOs to fill in — the
> EARS format, lifecycle, and traceability convention below are harness mechanics, identical across
> every project that opts a feature into SDD. Nothing here is project-specific: `install.sh` refreshes
> it on every install and keeps it out of git, so change it in the source toolkit, not here.

## What this is

A feature can require a **human-approved spec** before any code is written. This is **opt-in per
feature** (`sdd=1`) — every feature that doesn't opt in works exactly as it always has: `pending →
claim → in_progress → done`.

Opt a feature in with any of:
- `scripts/harness.sh add-feature --name <slug> --title <t> --sdd`
- a `features.seed.json` item with `"sdd": true`
- a Notion database checkbox property named `SDD` (if Notion intake is configured — see `AGENTS.md` §0)

## Lifecycle

```
pending ──claim-spec──> spec_drafting ──mark-spec-ready──> spec_ready
                                                                │
                                                  (human reviews the 3 files,
                                                   approves in conversation)
                                                                │
                                                                ▼
                                                          approve-spec
                                                                │
                                                                ▼
                                                    claim ──> in_progress ──log-out──> done
```

- **`scripts/harness.sh claim-spec [TARGET]`** — a `spec_author` subagent claims a `pending, sdd=1`
  feature, opening a session (mirrors `claim`).
- **`scripts/harness.sh mark-spec-ready`** — closes that session once
  `specs/<name>/{requirements.md,design.md,tasks.md}` all exist on disk; records requirement/task
  counts; moves the feature to `spec_ready`.
- **Revising a spec in review** — if the human asks for changes after `mark-spec-ready` (and before
  approval), the `spec_author` re-runs **`scripts/harness.sh claim-spec <target>`** (explicit target
  only): the feature goes back to `spec_drafting`, the revision is logged in a new session, and
  `mark-spec-ready` records the new counts. An already-approved spec can't be re-opened this way.
- **Human approval** — the user reads the 3 files and approves, in conversation. The leader then runs
  **`scripts/harness.sh approve-spec <target> --by <name>`** — this is the actual DB-recorded gate.
  Nothing mechanical can verify a human's judgment, but this at least proves *someone* explicitly
  recorded approval, with a timestamp and a name, rather than the leader silently skipping the step.
  It recounts requirements/tasks from the files at that moment, so the recorded counts are the ones the
  human approved.
- **`scripts/harness.sh claim [TARGET]`** — for an `sdd=1` feature, only succeeds once its spec is
  recorded `approved` (and the 3 files are still present on disk). From here the feature proceeds
  exactly like any non-SDD one: `implementer` then `reviewer`, then `log-out`.

## File layout

```
specs/<name>/
├── requirements.md   # WHAT is needed (EARS notation)
├── design.md         # HOW it will be built (technical decisions)
└── tasks.md          # Concrete, ordered steps to implement
```

`<name>` must match the feature's `name` field exactly (`scripts/harness.sh status`) — that's how
`claim`, `mark-spec-ready`, `init.sh`, and the reviewer all locate it. **`specs/` is git-tracked, like
`src/`/`tests/`** — never gitignored, never regenerated by `scripts/snapshot.sh` (unlike `state/`,
which is a disposable, wholesale-regenerated view of the database kept out of git, spec content here
is the actual source material).

## Requirement format: EARS

Every requirement in `requirements.md` gets a stable id (`R1`, `R2`, ...) and follows one of these five
patterns:

| Pattern | Template |
|---|---|
| Ubiquitous | `The system SHALL <action>.` |
| Event | `WHEN <trigger>, the system SHALL <action>.` |
| State | `WHILE <state>, the system SHALL <action>.` |
| Optional | `WHERE <optional feature>, the system SHALL <action>.` |
| Unwanted | `IF <unwanted event> THEN the system SHALL <action>.` |

Hard rules:
- **One requirement per business rule**, not one per clause. A business rule is a decision the user
  recognizes by name ("valid draft name", "bypass toggle"). Its variants (each error, each state) are
  rows of a case table inside that requirement, not separate `R<n>`s. Two behaviors the user would
  name differently are two requirements — never mix them.
- Every case must be verifiable: no soft verbs ("should probably", "handle appropriately",
  "supports"). If it can't be turned into a pass/fail check, sharpen it or move it to `design.md`.
- Existing behavior the feature touches but must not change is a **preservation** item (`P<n>`), not
  a requirement: it needs a test proving it still holds, never new code.

Example:

```markdown
## R1 — Export to file
WHEN the user runs `export`, the system SHALL write a JSON file to the given path.

## R2 — Valid draft name
WHEN the draft name changes, the system SHALL validate it and, while invalid, SHALL disable Export
and Send.

| Case | Input | Expected |
|---|---|---|
| a | empty | error `name_required` |
| b | contains `/` | error `name_unsupported` |
| c | matches an existing preset | error `name_duplicate` |
| d | any case above | Export and Send disabled |

## P1 — Source presets stay read-only
Opening a source preset still shows it read-only (existing behavior, test only).
```

Before this rule, R2 above would have been four requirements (one per error plus the disabled
buttons) — four test blocks, four traceability rows, four review checks for one decision.

## Scope limit

A spec may have at most **15 requirements** (`.harness.json::spec_max_requirements`, default 15;
0 disables). `mark-spec-ready` and `approve-spec` refuse a spec above it. Agent cost grows faster
than linearly with scope — every extra rule adds reads, edits and test runs to one ever-growing
context — so a feature that needs more is split, not squeezed.

The `spec_author` estimates scope **before** `claim-spec`. If the feature has more than 15
business rules, or its production code would clearly exceed the review budget (below), it writes
`progress/split_<name>.md` and reports `split-proposed`, without claiming:

```markdown
# Split proposal — <name>
Rules found: 22 (limit 15). Estimated production diff: ~1,100 lines (budget 400).

| Part | Name | Title | Rules | Est. code lines | Depends on |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | preset-duplicate | Duplicate a preset | 8 | ~350 | — |
| 2 | preset-edit-validation | Edit and validate the draft | 9 | ~400 | preset-duplicate |
| 3 | preset-export | Export and send | 5 | ~300 | preset-edit-validation |
```

The leader shows it to the user. On approval it creates each part with `add-feature --sdd
--depends-on <previous>` (plus a Notion card per part if configured) and supersedes the original
with `supersede <name> --by <first part>`. Each part then gets its own spec and session.

## Review budget (advisory)

About **400 production-code lines** per feature (`.harness.json::review_budget_lines`; 0 disables)
is what a person can review well in one pass. It is advisory: the split estimate above flags parts
over it, and `log-out` records a `DIFF: code=N, tests=M` entry for the feature and prints a `[WARN]`
when code exceeds it. Nothing blocks on it; the logged numbers calibrate future splits.

## `design.md`

Files to touch, new signatures, exceptions/error paths, and **at least one discarded alternative with a
stated reason it was rejected**. Reference `docs/architecture.md`/`docs/conventions.md` rather than
re-deriving this project's conventions from scratch — `design.md` should only need to explain choices
made *within* those boundaries, not restate them.

## `tasks.md`

Ordered checkboxes (`T1`, `T2`, ...), each tagged with the `R<n>`/`P<n>` id(s) it covers. Write the
failing test for a rule before the code that satisfies it:

```markdown
- [ ] T1 (R1) Add `test_export_default`, then `cmd_export`.
- [ ] T2 (R2) Add `test_draft_name_validation` (cases a–d), then the validator.
- [ ] T3 (P1) Add `test_source_preset_read_only` against the unchanged behavior.
```

The `implementer` checks these off in order as it works; the `reviewer` rejects the feature if any are
left `[ ]` without a documented justification in `progress/impl_<feature>.md`.

## Traceability

Every `R<n>` must be covered by **at least one concrete, currently-passing test**, and every case
row by an assertion — a table-driven test (`it.each`, `@pytest.mark.parametrize`, subtests) is the
natural shape: one test per rule, one row per case. Every `P<n>` needs one test proving the
existing behavior still holds. No padding tests beyond that. The `implementer` documents the
mapping in `progress/impl_<feature>.md`:

```markdown
## Traceability
- R1 → `test_export_default`
- R2 (a–d) → `test_draft_name_validation` (it.each, 4 rows)
- P1 → `test_source_preset_read_only`
```

The `reviewer` does not trust this claim — it independently confirms each named test exists and
actually exercises the requirement it's mapped to (see `.claude/agents/reviewer.md` / `.codex/agents/
reviewer.toml`, step 4b), and checks the same `R<n>` coverage as part of `CHECKPOINTS.md`'s C6 group.

## Coverage granularity

Every requirement still needs a concrete, currently-passing test. A compact
acceptance evidence table may group multiple requirements in one row when the
named test actually exercises each of them. State the grouping in design.md and
list every requirement id explicitly. A preservation requirement can use one
baseline comparison when it checks the complete required surface; separate rows
for each unchanged item are unnecessary. These choices reduce report repetition,
not the evidence required or the independent reviewer check.

An evidence index generated from names is only a navigation aid. It neither
executes tests nor establishes that they exercise the mapped requirements.

See `harness/instructions/coverage.md` for shared guidance on existing installs.
